<Doc>
    <Section title="Find which layer a click spends its time in">
        <Example
            id="layer-share"
            app="crm"
            title="`traceOp(page, op, body)` and `shares(trace, spec)` — one layer's part of a click"
            summary="Ranking five thousand deals takes a sort and a render. Its spec clicks the other ranking in Chromium and asks what part of the click the sort was."
            files={{
                'src/reports/lib/TopDeals.abide': topDealsSource,
                'src/reports/lib/topFive.ts': topFiveSource,
                'e2e/TopDeals.test.ts': topDealsSpecSource,
            }}
        >
            <TopDeals />
        </Example>

        <p>A faster sort saves at most the part of the click the sort was. Find that part before
            changing the sort. The code marks its layer with <code>performance.measure</code>, and the
            spec marks the whole click the same way, from the click to the frame that shows it.</p>

        <p><code>traceOp</code> records what Chromium did over that window, as a
            <code>Trace</code>: the user timing, and the style recalculations, layouts, paints and
            forced layouts. <code>shares</code> divides the layer by the op, as a
            <code>SharesSpec</code> names them, and hands back a <code>Shares</code>.
            <code>ceilingCpu</code> is the part of the processor's time the layer took.
            <code>ceilingObserved</code> is zero whenever the whole click fits in a frame, because a
            person sees the result at the same frame either way.</p>

        <p><code>measuresNamed</code> lists every measure of one name in a trace, and
            <code>durationOf</code> sums them. The trace is read over the DevTools protocol, so the
            spec is a Playwright test that runs in Chromium against the served app.</p>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
